1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,980
It's a truly extraordinary story, this, Tom.

2
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:03,400
So there are moments in the French Revolution

3
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:07,300
that feel like the purest melodrama on there.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:09,160
And this is absolutely one of them.

5
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:11,380
And it's a thriller, actually.

6
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:12,420
It's a really thrilling story.

7
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,720
And the climax, the way it unfolds,

8
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,360
changes the course of French history,

9
00:00:17,480 --> 00:00:18,920
arguably European history.

10
00:00:19,280 --> 00:00:23,920
This gamble that goes, spoiler alert, horribly, horribly wrong.

11
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:26,420
And I love it because it goes with the grain

12
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:28,280
of so much of what we know already

13
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:30,720
about some of the main players.

14
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:34,040
So Louis, Marie Antoinette, some of their hangers-on

15
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:37,080
who we've been hearing about throughout the series.

16
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,580
And they appear yet again.

17
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:40,300
So it's a very dramatic story.

18
00:00:40,560 --> 00:00:43,100
But of course, as you say, I mean, really,

19
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,620
its significance is the impact it will have

20
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:47,460
on the whole course of the revolution

21
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:48,800
and therefore of French history.

22
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:50,120
Yeah, definitely, definitely.

23
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:52,980
So let's remind ourselves where we got to last time.

24
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:54,500
So last time, actually, Tom,

25
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:56,400
you took us through an equally exciting

26
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:57,140
and extraordinary episode,

27
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,080
the Women's March on Versailles in October 1789.

28
00:01:01,420 --> 00:01:04,320
And we ended with the royal family being brought back

29
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:08,420
in that sort of an incredibly humiliating moment there, actually.

30
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:10,840
They're brought back in this great procession

31
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:13,440
by the Fishwives and by the National Guard

32
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:16,860
with the National Assembly eventually trailing behind them

33
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:20,220
to Paris, and they're installed in the Tuileries Palace.

34
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:21,800
So that was last time.

35
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:25,960
And now let's move on 12 months to the end of 1790.

36
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:29,140
And look at where the revolution has got to.

37
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:30,480
So the revolution has now been,

38
00:01:30,540 --> 00:01:32,500
the revolution proper, I suppose you could call it,

39
00:01:32,620 --> 00:01:34,500
has been going for more than a year.

40
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:36,160
It's more than a year after the fall of the Bastille,

41
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,640
the Estates General becoming the National Assembly,

42
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,940
and basically the deputies seizing sovereignty,

43
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:43,420
seizing power from the king.

44
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:47,040
And the focus of the story now is no longer in Versailles.

45
00:01:47,120 --> 00:01:49,560
Versailles has been shuttered, has been kind of closed up.

46
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:50,520
It's kind of become a museum.

47
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:51,000
It has.

48
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:51,640
Almost, hasn't it?

49
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:53,700
Yeah, and the focus is in Paris.

50
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:55,820
So if you'd gone to Paris,

51
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:58,800
as a visitor, you would have noticed straight away

52
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,440
at the end of 1790 how, for ordinary people,

53
00:02:02,190 --> 00:02:03,420
life looks different.

54
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:08,800
So there's a sort of unbridled sense of political freedom.

55
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:11,460
People are wearing tricolor cockades.

56
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,260
There are newspapers everywhere.

57
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:17,760
The people are singing patriotic songs, patriotic ballads.

58
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:20,880
They would do them, they would sing them in the interval

59
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:22,600
at the theatre and the opera.

60
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:23,540
So there's that.

61
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:26,180
The revolution is a daily experience.

62
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:30,800
It is a kind of living experience for Paris' hundreds of thousands of inhabitants.

63
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:34,140
And Dominic, this is, I think, when William Wordsworth,

64
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:35,960
the great romantic poet, goes to Paris,

65
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:39,160
and he writes his feelings about it, the famous lines,

66
00:02:39,700 --> 00:02:43,980
bliss was it in that dawn to be alive, but to be young was very heaven.

67
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:48,540
So that sense of joyous expectation of the entire world

68
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:50,640
on the cusp of a brilliant new dawn

69
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:53,960
is absolutely what is animating people in the streets, isn't it?

70
00:02:54,020 --> 00:02:54,980
It is, absolutely, yeah.

71
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:55,980
And there's a sense of,

72
00:02:56,900 --> 00:02:59,580
people pushing all the time at boundaries,

73
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:01,100
people saying the unsayable,

74
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:03,820
saying things they wouldn't have said a year ago or two years ago.

75
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,700
So it's a perfect example, actually,

76
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:10,140
of a city that is living politics every day.

77
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:12,940
And the tone is increasingly set by radical journalists.

78
00:03:13,060 --> 00:03:15,100
So you mentioned one, in fact, you mentioned a couple,

79
00:03:16,140 --> 00:03:18,300
Camille Desmoulins, Jean-Paul Marat.

80
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:20,500
And the tone of their stuff

81
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:23,340
is, I think, what we would now call populism.

82
00:03:23,540 --> 00:03:25,980
So they have, on the one hand, the people,

83
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:28,620
the nation, who are virtuous,

84
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:32,500
who are destined for greatness,

85
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:34,760
but have been frustrated up till now

86
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:37,420
by the machinations of conspirators.

87
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:38,540
These could be foreigners,

88
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:41,440
they could be priests, aristocrats.

89
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:45,060
And that sort of sense of a hardening,

90
00:03:45,100 --> 00:03:47,240
that goes along with, I think,

91
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:51,140
with the egalitarian democratic excitement that you get.

92
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:55,380
But there's also a sense that you also get in the National Assembly,

93
00:03:56,420 --> 00:03:59,320
of excitement at the limits that you're pushing,

94
00:03:59,440 --> 00:04:03,060
and the sense that actually everything could be new.

95
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:06,380
So all traces of history could be effaced.

96
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,180
France could be completely reborn,

97
00:04:09,320 --> 00:04:11,020
and perhaps the world with it.

98
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:15,360
And I guess that that sense of excitement

99
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:19,060
is kind of what is new about the politics of the situation.

100
00:04:19,420 --> 00:04:20,300
Yeah, I think so.

101
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:21,000
I think so.

102
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:23,420
And I think it's a sense of radical excitement

103
00:04:23,420 --> 00:04:26,400
that goes beyond anything that's been seen in previous...

104
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:28,620
So, of course, there's been a degree of excitement

105
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:29,580
in the English Revolution,

106
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,060
in the 17th century of the American Revolution.

107
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:37,040
But this is the sense that everything that went before,

108
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:38,920
you can get rid of it.

109
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:39,980
You know better now.

110
00:04:40,280 --> 00:04:41,500
You can do something new.

111
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:43,720
And, of course, for the deputies of the National Assembly,

112
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:44,680
that's both very exciting,

113
00:04:44,760 --> 00:04:45,960
but also potentially very exhausting.

114
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:47,940
Because they have been working...

115
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:48,860
This is a lot of work to do, isn't it?

116
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:51,540
Yeah, they have been working now for the best part of two years

117
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:54,200
on a constitution, on a new framework for France.

118
00:04:54,260 --> 00:04:55,280
And they're rethinking everything.

119
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:57,940
Government, law.

120
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:01,300
I mean, you talked before about the most incendiary

121
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:02,000
of all these things,

122
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:03,740
which is the relationship of church and state.

123
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:06,000
So they're...

124
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,200
And all of these things have massive repercussions

125
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:08,900
in the countryside.

126
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:10,940
And they're getting letters back the whole time.

127
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:11,840
They're deluged.

128
00:05:11,960 --> 00:05:12,640
It's actually...

129
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:15,040
The French Revolution is, if nothing else,

130
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:16,120
it's a story about paperwork.

131
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:19,700
So it's a story about young men basically being overwhelmed

132
00:05:19,700 --> 00:05:22,140
with enormous quantities of paperwork.

133
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:24,980
And, actually, the people at the National Assembly,

134
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:26,200
of course, they are very excited,

135
00:05:26,200 --> 00:05:28,320
but they're also unbelievably tired.

136
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:30,880
So historians who talk about this period

137
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:33,900
notice that in their letters they say all the time

138
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,440
that they're suffering from massive headaches,

139
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:38,000
they're not eating, they can't sleep,

140
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:39,300
they are just overwhelmed.

141
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:40,640
And, actually, more and more,

142
00:05:42,240 --> 00:05:44,680
it's a hard core who are driving the politics.

143
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:48,400
So there are about 1,200 people in the National Assembly,

144
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:52,060
but only 400 of them are actually still turning up.

145
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,740
The nobles and a lot of the priests are now staying away.

146
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:55,480
Because...

147
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:57,640
And is that an expression of hostility to what is happening?

148
00:05:57,880 --> 00:05:57,960
Yes.

149
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:58,980
Or is it nervousness?

150
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:01,680
Or is it just they don't really identify with what's happening?

151
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:02,700
I think it's all of those things.

152
00:06:02,820 --> 00:06:05,100
I think they're worried about the way it's going.

153
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:07,280
I think many of them are outraged

154
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:08,680
at the course it has already taken.

155
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:13,320
And I think there's a sense that suddenly these people,

156
00:06:13,820 --> 00:06:14,680
the old guard, if you like,

157
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:16,060
are in a world that they don't understand.

158
00:06:16,360 --> 00:06:18,280
And they no longer want to be part of it.

159
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:20,320
And so they've gone back often to their states

160
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:22,860
in La France Profonde.

161
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:24,520
There is also a sense, though, isn't there,

162
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:26,780
that even as they are pushing into a bright new future,

163
00:06:26,920 --> 00:06:29,200
they are also increasingly starting to look

164
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:35,460
to ancient exemplars, which are Republican exemplars.

165
00:06:35,480 --> 00:06:35,900
That's true.

166
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,380
And particularly in the kind of the clubs

167
00:06:37,380 --> 00:06:38,500
that we talked about, the Jacopin Club,

168
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:40,120
at the end of the previous episode.

169
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,480
The exemplars are becoming Republican, aren't they?

170
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:47,760
Well, so the Jacopin Club that you ended with last time,

171
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:50,320
officially the Society of the Friends of the Constitution

172
00:06:50,320 --> 00:06:51,640
that meets in this old monastery,

173
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:53,040
very close to the National Assembly.

174
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:56,200
If you went into the Jacopin Club,

175
00:06:56,280 --> 00:07:01,160
they would have busts of Brutus, Cato, Benjamin Franklin.

176
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:04,520
They had framed copies of the Declaration of the Rights of Man.

177
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:06,600
There is a sort of sense that you're in,

178
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:09,940
I mean, it's a kind of metropolitan elite, dare I say.

179
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:10,920
Sure.

180
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:11,700
But it's a Republican.

181
00:07:12,040 --> 00:07:12,800
Yeah, it is.

182
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:14,880
Brutus and Cato are both famous people

183
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:17,140
who fought against an autocrat.

184
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:20,060
But they don't see themselves as Republicans at this stage.

185
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:20,940
That's the interesting thing.

186
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:23,380
They're almost unconsciously Republicans, I would say.

187
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:24,140
Actually,

188
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:24,940
on the clubs,

189
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:28,240
the Jacopin Club is now at the heart.

190
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:29,420
There's a network of them.

191
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:31,000
It's a bit sort of Soho House.

192
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:35,400
So they've opened basically subsidiaries all across France.

193
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:37,900
There are about 400 of them by the beginning of 1791.

194
00:07:38,080 --> 00:07:38,360
With spas.

195
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:39,800
Yes, right.

196
00:07:40,020 --> 00:07:40,760
Swimming pools.

197
00:07:41,060 --> 00:07:41,200
Yeah.

198
00:07:41,580 --> 00:07:43,500
Well, you go there and they look very similar.

199
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:44,900
Hang out with supermodels.

200
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:45,320
It's exactly.

201
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:47,260
And dare I say,

202
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:52,460
the influences of the time go to the local Jacopin Club

203
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:53,260
because that is where,

204
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:54,120
you know,

205
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:58,920
aspirational upwardly mobile the people with momentum

206
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:01,400
with social and cultural momentum where they go.

207
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,500
But it's also a sign of a kind of factionalism

208
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:05,820
and have a kind of radicalization,

209
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:08,280
a radical ratchet because there are lots of other clubs competing

210
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:10,640
to outflank the Jacopin on the left.

211
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:13,300
And the most famous one of this is a club called the Society

212
00:08:13,300 --> 00:08:14,240
of the Rights of Man,

213
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:15,660
which is called the Cordelia Club.

214
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:19,000
This meets on the left bank near the Latin Quarter.

215
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:22,720
It's the sort of artistic publishing journalistic district.

216
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:28,980
The tone is set there at the Cordelia by people like Demoulin and Mara.

217
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,480
And they are really pushing two things.

218
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:34,500
One is saying the revolution needs to go much further democracy,

219
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:35,820
egalitarianism,

220
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:38,020
the people's rights and all that stuff.

221
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:41,800
And then the other side of it is that very aggressive Populist side.

222
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:44,840
So that it's at the Cordelia that people are going on about conspiracies

223
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:45,540
the whole time.

224
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:46,520
And Dominic,

225
00:08:46,540 --> 00:08:48,720
just to ask just the issue of democracy,

226
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,860
but because that then begs the question of which I'm sure people have been

227
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:57,300
wondering about who actually has the vote in all this.

228
00:08:57,500 --> 00:09:01,920
The Constitution as it's being structured has come up with the notion of active

229
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:02,960
and passive citizens,

230
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:03,480
hasn't it?

231
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:03,580
Yeah,

232
00:09:03,640 --> 00:09:03,900
that's right.

233
00:09:04,020 --> 00:09:11,000
Active citizens are basically males over 25 who pay a certain amount of money

234
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,220
in tax or whatever each year and they have voting rights and everyone else doesn't.

235
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:17,600
And that of course includes women.

236
00:09:17,980 --> 00:09:23,740
So there is scope there for a left would push inherent in the kind of

237
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:25,220
model of democracy that's being structured.

238
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:25,920
There is absolutely.

239
00:09:26,900 --> 00:09:31,260
So that's one of the things that is as it were pushing the revolution a sense of

240
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:34,500
democratic excitement in a sense that you can actually there are always more

241
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:35,340
boundaries to cross.

242
00:09:36,460 --> 00:09:39,440
But the other thing Simon Sharma says of these clubs.

243
00:09:39,620 --> 00:09:43,140
So there are these kind of fraternal societies as they call that are associated

244
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:47,800
with Cordelia that are some of them in some ways become even more radical and

245
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:51,440
he says their rhetoric was Russo with a horse voice and sharpened with bloody

246
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,540
minded impatience and that bloody.

247
00:09:53,740 --> 00:09:59,160
Minded impatience is very important because in late 1790 early 1791 bread prices

248
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:00,100
are still very high.

249
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:02,940
And in fact inflation has gone through the roof because the government is now

250
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:05,940
printing a lot of paper money a senior they called.

251
00:10:06,800 --> 00:10:12,260
So when you have high inflation you have labor unrest because people say their

252
00:10:12,260 --> 00:10:14,700
wages aren't high enough to meet their their outgoings.

253
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:22,320
So there's a wave of strikes through the winter 17 1991 in Paris that basically

254
00:10:23,740 --> 00:10:28,060
creates a it adds to the sense of unrest and anarchy and things being out of

255
00:10:28,060 --> 00:10:32,080
control and not only does it have you know, it's not good for the economy.

256
00:10:32,520 --> 00:10:35,700
It's not good for the deputies in the National Assembly's blood pressure because

257
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:39,360
they're living in the midst of all this they're living in Paris and they look

258
00:10:39,360 --> 00:10:42,220
out of their window when they get up in the morning and they see crowds on the

259
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:46,380
streets people shouting people saying where are the fruits that we were expecting

