963
00:48:34,220 --> 00:48:36,780
And then he looks at the man sitting next to her.

964
00:48:37,020 --> 00:48:41,460
And he's this big, fat, heavy-set man who he hasn't seen before.

965
00:48:41,740 --> 00:48:44,420
But he had seen Marie Antoinette at Versailles when he was a cavalryman.

966
00:48:44,520 --> 00:48:45,680
But he hasn't seen this man.

967
00:48:45,800 --> 00:48:46,980
But he recognizes the man.

968
00:48:47,340 --> 00:48:50,400
Because the man has the same face that is on the new inventory.

969
00:48:50,420 --> 00:48:51,140
Inflationary.

970
00:48:51,140 --> 00:48:51,520
Paper.

971
00:48:51,700 --> 00:48:52,140
Money.

972
00:48:52,540 --> 00:48:53,880
It's Louis XVI.

973
00:48:55,520 --> 00:48:57,160
The horses are now finished.

974
00:48:57,380 --> 00:48:57,820
Change.

975
00:48:58,140 --> 00:49:01,900
And before Drouet can do anything, the carriage rattles away out of the yard.

976
00:49:02,180 --> 00:49:03,500
And he's just standing there.

977
00:49:03,620 --> 00:49:06,720
And he says to the people around him, that was the king and queen.

978
00:49:07,060 --> 00:49:08,800
I know that was the king and queen.

979
00:49:10,060 --> 00:49:12,320
And Saint-Menu is a small place.

980
00:49:13,100 --> 00:49:14,240
People start to gather.

981
00:49:14,380 --> 00:49:16,420
And Drouet says, I know it.

982
00:49:16,860 --> 00:49:18,100
There's no doubt in my mind.

983
00:49:18,160 --> 00:49:18,800
That's who they were.

984
00:49:18,800 --> 00:49:20,520
And the local bigwigs appear.

985
00:49:20,940 --> 00:49:22,320
And they're like, what are they doing?

986
00:49:22,860 --> 00:49:31,980
Now, in this world where the conspiracy theories have been circulating all this time, they immediately say, he's going to get a foreign army.

987
00:49:32,340 --> 00:49:33,500
That's what he's going to do.

988
00:49:33,560 --> 00:49:36,780
He's going to invade this country and attack our revolution.

989
00:49:36,860 --> 00:49:37,780
Stop our revolution.

990
00:49:38,060 --> 00:49:38,920
They might not be wrong.

991
00:49:39,160 --> 00:49:39,900
Well, they're right.

992
00:49:40,000 --> 00:49:41,220
Yeah, they're absolutely right.

993
00:49:41,640 --> 00:49:45,180
And the town bigwigs say, who's the fastest rider?

994
00:49:45,320 --> 00:49:48,120
It's Drouet, who had once been a cavalryman.

995
00:49:48,120 --> 00:49:50,460
They say, you would get your mate Jean-Guillaume.

996
00:49:50,720 --> 00:49:51,820
You go after them.

997
00:49:51,900 --> 00:49:52,440
You must stop.

998
00:49:52,500 --> 00:49:53,060
You must stop.

999
00:49:53,180 --> 00:49:54,960
Tell people in the next town and stop them.

1000
00:49:56,080 --> 00:49:57,900
The next town is a town called Varennes.

1001
00:49:58,940 --> 00:50:03,280
At 11 o'clock, the royal coach approaches Varennes.

1002
00:50:03,400 --> 00:50:06,220
It is an hour and a half ahead of Drouet.

1003
00:50:06,400 --> 00:50:07,860
So they have a bit of time to play with.

1004
00:50:07,940 --> 00:50:10,800
Varennes is about 1,500 people in the Argonne forest.

1005
00:50:12,300 --> 00:50:13,800
Don't forget, they don't have their escort.

1006
00:50:14,080 --> 00:50:18,100
Now, they were expecting that the army would leave horses for them on the outskirts of Varennes.

1007
00:50:18,100 --> 00:50:21,140
But of course, the message has gone around.

1008
00:50:21,280 --> 00:50:22,360
The treasure is not coming today.

1009
00:50:22,760 --> 00:50:24,660
So those horses are nowhere to be seen.

1010
00:50:24,960 --> 00:50:26,560
The royal bodyguards get down.

1011
00:50:26,680 --> 00:50:27,340
They look for them.

1012
00:50:27,400 --> 00:50:29,200
They even knock on a few doors.

1013
00:50:29,460 --> 00:50:30,140
No joy.

1014
00:50:30,260 --> 00:50:30,720
No horses.

1015
00:50:31,400 --> 00:50:36,080
There's a lot of dithering and arguing because the people with the current horses don't want to go on.

1016
00:50:36,160 --> 00:50:38,380
They don't want to push the horses beyond the limits of their endurance.

1017
00:50:38,480 --> 00:50:42,060
They've been specifically told not to go past Varennes.

1018
00:50:42,300 --> 00:50:43,620
So there's then a bit of an argument.

1019
00:50:43,660 --> 00:50:47,860
The drivers don't want to keep going until they've had a rest, until they've fed the horses and all of this stuff.

1020
00:50:48,100 --> 00:51:01,120
And while this argument is going on, by the side of the road, Drouet and Guillaume ride right past them without even noticing them towards Varennes.

1021
00:51:02,800 --> 00:51:05,280
Finally, the bodyguards get back onto the coach.

1022
00:51:05,400 --> 00:51:07,620
They agree they'll go into Varennes and they'll see what they can do.

1023
00:51:07,820 --> 00:51:11,740
And the carriages roll into Varennes, which is in total darkness now.

1024
00:51:11,820 --> 00:51:13,200
We're virtually midnight.

1025
00:51:14,040 --> 00:51:17,580
And then as they approach this inn in the center of the town,

1026
00:51:17,580 --> 00:51:21,460
the inn of the Golden Arm, they can hear people shouting, voices.

1027
00:51:21,720 --> 00:51:28,700
Somebody's shouting, fire, fire, which is the typical, the classic thing you shout to attract attention with any kind of emergency.

1028
00:51:29,460 --> 00:51:35,320
And Madame de Tourzel said later, she said, when we heard people shouting that, we thought we'd been betrayed.

1029
00:51:35,540 --> 00:51:40,600
We drove down the street with a feeling of sadness and distress that can scarcely be described.

1030
00:51:40,960 --> 00:51:46,900
And as they get to the Golden Arm, there are men standing there who stop the carriages and they think, oh, no.

1031
00:51:46,900 --> 00:51:47,860
Oh, no.

1032
00:51:48,120 --> 00:51:51,220
So what had actually happened was that Drouet had got there before them.

1033
00:51:51,460 --> 00:51:54,340
He had shouted at the burst into the inn.

1034
00:51:54,720 --> 00:51:59,300
And he had said the king and queen are coming for the sake of the country, for the sake of the revolution.

1035
00:52:00,320 --> 00:52:01,380
They must be stopped.

1036
00:52:02,580 --> 00:52:07,120
People aroused the local councilors, the local National Guard and the chief magistrate,

1037
00:52:07,160 --> 00:52:13,340
who is a grocer with the excellent name of Jean-Baptiste Sauce, or as he would now call himself, Sauce.

1038
00:52:15,140 --> 00:52:16,880
So they're the people who are standing there.

1039
00:52:16,900 --> 00:52:18,360
They're standing outside the inn when the carriages arrive.

1040
00:52:18,860 --> 00:52:20,380
You can picture the scene.

1041
00:52:20,760 --> 00:52:22,840
People holding torches aloft in the darkness.

1042
00:52:23,200 --> 00:52:24,440
They ask for their papers.

1043
00:52:24,620 --> 00:52:27,960
They hand over the papers in the name of Baroness de Corfe and go.

1044
00:52:28,720 --> 00:52:34,060
And actually, Sauce and the others say that these papers seem perfectly in order.

1045
00:52:35,020 --> 00:52:37,420
Maybe we should let them go on.

1046
00:52:37,480 --> 00:52:42,020
They're kind of muttering while Louis and Marie-Antoinette are waiting in terror in the carriage.

1047
00:52:42,520 --> 00:52:46,260
And Drouet says, I swear this is the king and queen.

1048
00:52:46,980 --> 00:52:48,260
You can't let them go.

1049
00:52:48,360 --> 00:52:49,460
You absolutely can't let them go.

1050
00:52:49,840 --> 00:52:55,880
So the bigwigs of the town, the town fathers say to themselves, why don't we just play for time and wait till the morning?

1051
00:52:56,320 --> 00:52:59,820
So they say to the travelers, look, it's very late.

1052
00:52:59,960 --> 00:53:03,520
We can't really, you know, we'll need more time to check your documents.

1053
00:53:03,640 --> 00:53:04,340
We'll do it in the morning.

1054
00:53:04,720 --> 00:53:06,620
Also, the road is very dangerous at night.

1055
00:53:06,680 --> 00:53:07,920
You'd be better off going in the morning anyway.

1056
00:53:08,020 --> 00:53:08,860
Why don't you stay here?

1057
00:53:09,200 --> 00:53:11,800
Jean-Baptiste Sauce says, do you know what?

1058
00:53:11,860 --> 00:53:14,060
You can stay in my house above the grocer's shop.

1059
00:53:14,600 --> 00:53:15,900
I've got a bedroom for you.

1060
00:53:16,400 --> 00:53:21,300
And very, very reluctantly, they get down from the carriage and they go to the grocer's house.

1061
00:53:21,980 --> 00:53:25,960
And the two children, who are, of course, exhausted, are put to bed.

1062
00:53:26,940 --> 00:53:27,920
And then they wait.

1063
00:53:29,120 --> 00:53:31,480
While Sauce is leaving them there, he goes down.

1064
00:53:31,560 --> 00:53:34,740
He says, there's a bloke in the town, actually, who's seen the king and queen.

1065
00:53:34,980 --> 00:53:35,660
A judge.

1066
00:53:35,940 --> 00:53:37,860
Because he married a woman from Versailles.

1067
00:53:38,900 --> 00:53:39,620
Monsieur Destes.

1068
00:53:40,320 --> 00:53:41,180
I'll go and get him.

1069
00:53:41,260 --> 00:53:44,420
He goes and wakes him up, bangs on his door, brings him to his house.

1070
00:53:44,420 --> 00:53:49,300
This guy, Destes, comes up the stairs, goes into the bedroom.

1071
00:53:49,800 --> 00:53:56,800
As soon as he sees Louis XVI, he drops to his knees and he says, oh, your highness, your majesty.

1072
00:53:57,940 --> 00:54:03,620
And Louis stands up and he says, yes, I am your king.

1073
00:54:04,060 --> 00:54:06,840
I have come to live among you, my faithful children.

1074
00:54:06,920 --> 00:54:08,060
I will never abandon you.

1075
00:54:08,800 --> 00:54:10,640
And then he does this incredible thing.

1076
00:54:10,820 --> 00:54:14,180
Timothy Tackett says, he took the members of the municipal council.

1077
00:54:14,180 --> 00:54:16,360
He bounced in his arms one by one and embraced them.

1078
00:54:16,420 --> 00:54:17,660
And he told them his story.

1079
00:54:17,940 --> 00:54:21,400
And he said, I've been living in the capital, surrounded by daggers and bayonets.

1080
00:54:21,680 --> 00:54:26,940
But I've come to the countryside to seek the freedom and tranquility that you all enjoy.

1081
00:54:27,700 --> 00:54:28,520
So very Rousseau.

1082
00:54:28,700 --> 00:54:29,440
Yeah, totally.

1083
00:54:29,780 --> 00:54:31,440
But he's basically saying, please.

1084
00:54:31,940 --> 00:54:32,640
Yeah, of course.

1085
00:54:32,720 --> 00:54:33,040
Help me.

1086
00:54:33,360 --> 00:54:36,440
My life and the lives of my family are in danger.

1087
00:54:36,580 --> 00:54:41,280
But he's framing it in a way that is with the grain of kind of revolutionary sentiment.

1088
00:54:41,560 --> 00:54:41,820
Yeah.

1089
00:54:41,820 --> 00:54:42,920
That's the extraordinary feat.

1090
00:54:43,120 --> 00:54:43,280
Yeah.

1091
00:54:43,360 --> 00:54:48,060
I mean, not bad, actually, in the context of the absolute meltdown of all his plans.

1092
00:54:48,180 --> 00:54:48,660
Well, do you know what?

1093
00:54:48,980 --> 00:54:54,620
It almost works because they are dumbstruck that the king.

1094
00:54:54,800 --> 00:54:56,000
I mean, they would never.

1095
00:54:56,760 --> 00:55:00,360
I mean, he's in the upstairs bedroom of a grocer's house.

1096
00:55:00,680 --> 00:55:02,280
It's an inconceivable.

1097
00:55:02,380 --> 00:55:04,120
I mean, 1789.

1098
00:55:05,140 --> 00:55:10,520
This would have been beyond the realms of the wildest fantasy that this would happen.

1099
00:55:10,520 --> 00:55:17,140
And they are shaking with nerves and fear and emotion to be in the presence of the king.

1100
00:55:18,200 --> 00:55:22,200
And they say they will help him for a moment.

1101
00:55:22,640 --> 00:55:28,720
They say, of course, of course, if necessary, we will accompany you east ourselves.

1102
00:55:28,980 --> 00:55:32,540
You know, if that's what it takes, then they all go downstairs.

1103
00:55:33,140 --> 00:55:40,280
And it's actually while they're going downstairs that they're like, actually, maybe we shouldn't do this.

1104
00:55:40,280 --> 00:55:49,300
And Timothy Tackett, who I mentioned already, his book is brilliant on this because he says, you know, you have to look at Varenne itself, what had happened in Varenne to make sense of this.

1105
00:55:49,360 --> 00:55:51,740
Varenne is a completely nondescript, ordinary town.

1106
00:55:52,340 --> 00:56:00,020
But in towns like Varenne, there had been a degree of politicization that had never been before since the revolution began.

1107
00:56:00,480 --> 00:56:02,880
They have sent delegates to the National Assembly.

1108
00:56:03,040 --> 00:56:03,860
They've had meetings.

1109
00:56:04,220 --> 00:56:08,820
They have their own little Jacobin club in Varenne that all the bigwigs belong to.

1110
00:56:08,820 --> 00:56:15,260
And also, for two years, they have been subject to what you talked about, Tom, le grand peur, the great fear.

1111
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:18,040
Fears of brigands and foreign armies.

1112
00:56:18,400 --> 00:56:20,460
Well, particularly being close to the frontier.

1113
00:56:20,720 --> 00:56:21,080
Yeah.

1114
00:56:21,160 --> 00:56:25,200
You know, they would be in the front line of any invasion coming.

1115
00:56:25,640 --> 00:56:26,080
Exactly.

1116
00:56:26,360 --> 00:56:31,600
And so they say, actually, actually, no, this is a terrible situation.

1117
00:56:31,920 --> 00:56:33,900
Like, maybe he's raising a foreign army.

1118
00:56:34,280 --> 00:56:35,660
And they start to panic.

1119
00:56:36,040 --> 00:56:38,480
And they say, finally, ring the tocsin.

1120
00:56:38,820 --> 00:56:39,340
Ring the bells.

1121
00:56:39,860 --> 00:56:40,820
Call the alarm.

1122
00:56:41,040 --> 00:56:42,240
Now, this is a terrible situation.

1123
00:56:43,200 --> 00:56:47,980
And suddenly, it's 1 o'clock in the morning, and the church bells are tolling the tocsin.

1124
00:56:48,880 --> 00:56:52,640
People are banging on drums, shouting, fire, fire, alarm, alarm.

1125
00:56:53,200 --> 00:56:54,640
People are pouring into the streets.

1126
00:56:54,700 --> 00:56:56,420
They run to the town hall to get guns.

1127
00:56:57,380 --> 00:57:02,580
And people are sending messages to the local villages, to arms, to arms.

1128
00:57:02,800 --> 00:57:04,900
You know, everything's kicking off.

1129
00:57:05,700 --> 00:57:08,800
And, of course, Louis and Marie Antoinette would be upstairs in that.

1130
00:57:08,800 --> 00:57:14,560
In that room, listening to all this, and just realizing that it's all going horrendously,

1131
00:57:14,620 --> 00:57:15,520
horrendously wrong.

1132
00:57:15,740 --> 00:57:19,240
So if we just fast forward a few hours, because I know Theo doesn't want this episode to run

1133
00:57:19,240 --> 00:57:20,460
for six hours in itself.

1134
00:57:21,880 --> 00:57:26,980
When you get to dawn, by dawn, the street outside the grocery is absolutely rammed with

1135
00:57:26,980 --> 00:57:27,300
people.

1136
00:57:27,500 --> 00:57:28,780
Militia bands, basically.

1137
00:57:28,800 --> 00:57:33,820
People with pikes, and with sticks, and with battered old muskets, and all this kind of

1138
00:57:33,820 --> 00:57:34,060
thing.

1139
00:57:34,260 --> 00:57:36,400
Louis and Marie Antoinette have not slept a wink.

1140
00:57:36,480 --> 00:57:37,520
They're in terrible state.

1141
00:57:37,520 --> 00:57:38,940
The kids have been asleep, I guess.

1142
00:57:39,260 --> 00:57:43,060
But about daybreak, Sauce and the other bigwigs come back to Louis.

1143
00:57:43,200 --> 00:57:45,640
And they say, actually, we've changed our mind.

1144
00:57:45,820 --> 00:57:46,980
We can't let you go on.

1145
00:57:47,180 --> 00:57:50,600
They say, we're like members of a great family who've just found their father.

1146
00:57:50,660 --> 00:57:52,520
But we fear we may lose him again.

1147
00:57:53,860 --> 00:57:56,000
And they say, you know, everybody loves you.

1148
00:57:56,880 --> 00:57:58,500
You're in everybody's hearts.

1149
00:57:58,680 --> 00:58:00,280
But your place is in Paris.

1150
00:58:00,520 --> 00:58:02,060
And we want you to go back to Paris.

1151
00:58:02,600 --> 00:58:06,260
And Louis and Marie Antoinette, in desperate, you can just picture the scene.

1152
00:58:06,260 --> 00:58:07,000
Please let us go.

1153
00:58:07,080 --> 00:58:09,060
Yeah, they're desperately arguing with them.

1154
00:58:09,100 --> 00:58:11,100
Please let us, you know, you have to let us go.

1155
00:58:11,140 --> 00:58:11,880
We'll die in Paris.

1156
00:58:12,740 --> 00:58:18,440
And then at 6 o'clock in the morning, two men arrive from Paris, and they're couriers

1157
00:58:18,440 --> 00:58:21,000
with a message from General Lafayette.

1158
00:58:22,020 --> 00:58:28,740
The morning of the 21st, when the news had broken that the palace was deserted, Lafayette

1159
00:58:28,740 --> 00:58:31,440
and the National Assembly had sent messages all over France.

1160
00:58:31,760 --> 00:58:32,420
Stop them.

1161
00:58:32,920 --> 00:58:33,600
Intercept them.

1162
00:58:33,700 --> 00:58:34,880
Do not let them get away.

1163
00:58:34,880 --> 00:58:39,020
And they hand these messages, these decrees, the couriers, to the royal family.

1164
00:58:39,160 --> 00:58:41,620
Marie Antoinette just spits out.

1165
00:58:41,640 --> 00:58:43,060
She says, what insolence?

1166
00:58:43,240 --> 00:58:45,840
And she throws the message to the ground.

1167
00:58:46,180 --> 00:58:49,680
But a very revealing little sign of their two characters.

1168
00:58:50,220 --> 00:58:51,600
Louis doesn't react.

1169
00:58:51,680 --> 00:58:52,240
He's not angry.

1170
00:58:52,800 --> 00:58:54,660
He's just tired and sad.

1171
00:58:54,900 --> 00:58:57,840
And he says, there's no longer a king in France.

1172
00:58:58,200 --> 00:59:01,660
So at 7.30, they're led downstairs, and they're put back into these carriages.

1173
00:59:01,920 --> 00:59:03,520
Huge crowd standing around.

1174
00:59:03,520 --> 00:59:06,900
And this is worse than when they were brought from Versailles to Paris.

1175
00:59:07,160 --> 00:59:07,540
Oh, totally.

1176
00:59:07,680 --> 00:59:10,060
Because this is, they've failed.

1177
00:59:10,360 --> 00:59:15,560
They've been, I mean, the October thing in 1789 was humiliating.

1178
00:59:15,820 --> 00:59:18,440
But this is, they've been, they're guilty.

1179
00:59:18,760 --> 00:59:18,960
Caught.

1180
00:59:19,040 --> 00:59:19,560
They've been caught.

1181
00:59:19,920 --> 00:59:20,780
That's the thing.

1182
00:59:20,940 --> 00:59:21,940
They come down.

1183
00:59:22,160 --> 00:59:24,140
The crowd's all pressing around them.

1184
00:59:24,160 --> 00:59:25,420
People staring at them.

1185
00:59:25,620 --> 00:59:26,260
All this.

1186
00:59:26,300 --> 00:59:28,180
And people start to shout, vive la nation.

1187
00:59:28,800 --> 00:59:30,160
Go back to Paris.

1188
00:59:30,520 --> 00:59:31,580
And all this stuff.

1189
00:59:31,880 --> 00:59:32,800
But not vive le roi.

1190
00:59:32,800 --> 00:59:33,540
Not vive le roi.

1191
00:59:34,720 --> 00:59:39,680
The journey west, they then head west again with this huge crowd surrounding them.

1192
00:59:39,780 --> 00:59:42,060
It's a four-day journey this time.

1193
00:59:42,620 --> 00:59:44,220
It's very tiring.

1194
00:59:44,380 --> 00:59:45,560
It's very hot.

1195
00:59:45,900 --> 00:59:53,780
It's incredibly dusty because the great weight of the people surrounding them brings up loads of dust from these kind of roads.

1196
00:59:54,120 --> 01:00:02,680
And all the old kind of abusive canards that have been aimed at both Marie Antoinette and Louis.

1197
01:00:02,680 --> 01:00:04,340
Are kind of wheeled out again, aren't they?

1198
01:00:04,380 --> 01:00:07,380
So, Marie Antoinette is this voracious vampire.

1199
01:00:07,760 --> 01:00:08,720
Sexually predatory.

1200
01:00:09,000 --> 01:00:10,260
Been sleeping around with everyone.

1201
01:00:10,460 --> 01:00:12,920
And Louis is the kind of feeble cuckold.

1202
01:00:13,220 --> 01:00:18,920
And their characters are now absolutely being set in that kind of abusive cast.

1203
01:00:19,380 --> 01:00:20,060
Totally they are.

1204
01:00:20,120 --> 01:00:25,340
Because people are, I mean, if you think about, they're in this carriage, this kind of airless, horrible environment.

1205
01:00:25,920 --> 01:00:26,960
Day after day.

1206
01:00:27,380 --> 01:00:28,420
At least they've got their chamber pots.

1207
01:00:28,560 --> 01:00:28,880
Yeah.

1208
01:00:29,420 --> 01:00:29,860
Exactly.

1209
01:00:30,120 --> 01:00:31,060
And their picnic equipment.

1210
01:00:31,060 --> 01:00:36,780
And they are surrounded by thousands of people who, as you say, are shouting abuse through the windows.

1211
01:00:38,040 --> 01:00:38,920
Throwing stones.

1212
01:00:39,200 --> 01:00:43,580
And the royal bodyguards, the three nobles in the yellow coats are still sitting on the top.

1213
01:00:43,740 --> 01:00:47,580
And people are like hurling earth at them and dung and all this kind of thing.

1214
01:00:47,660 --> 01:00:47,760
Dead cats.

1215
01:00:47,760 --> 01:00:48,400
Dead cats, exactly.

1216
01:00:48,720 --> 01:00:51,320
So, it's a really sort of horrible scene.

1217
01:00:51,480 --> 01:00:53,580
There is actually one moment of violence.

1218
01:00:53,880 --> 01:00:59,660
So, outside Saint-Domingue, a local nobleman, a young count called Dompierre.

1219
01:00:59,660 --> 01:01:03,080
Tries to ride up to the carriage to shout, long live the king.

1220
01:01:03,420 --> 01:01:06,240
You know, he wants to show his solidarity with them.

1221
01:01:06,600 --> 01:01:08,220
The crowd drive him off.

1222
01:01:08,420 --> 01:01:12,000
And he rides off into the fields.

1223
01:01:12,340 --> 01:01:13,820
Louis is watching all this in the windows.

1224
01:01:14,700 --> 01:01:16,740
His own peasants pursue him.

1225
01:01:16,820 --> 01:01:19,380
They shoot him, drag him off his horse and then stab him to death.

1226
01:01:19,700 --> 01:01:22,100
With their kind of knives and pitchforks and stuff.

1227
01:01:22,200 --> 01:01:23,380
So, that must have helped set Louis.

1228
01:01:23,700 --> 01:01:24,720
Set his mind at rest.

1229
01:01:25,260 --> 01:01:25,700
Yeah.

1230
01:01:25,700 --> 01:01:30,460
On the second day, they were joined by three men from the National Assembly.

1231
01:01:30,900 --> 01:01:32,260
There was a monarchist deputy.

1232
01:01:32,460 --> 01:01:37,720
The National Assembly agreed they would send three people of different ideological persuasions to ride back with them.

1233
01:01:37,780 --> 01:01:39,380
A monarchist, a radical.

1234
01:01:39,620 --> 01:01:41,780
And this guy, Barnav, that we mentioned.

1235
01:01:41,840 --> 01:01:46,400
The guy from Grenoble who's the kind of the big man in the National Assembly.

1236
01:01:46,500 --> 01:01:48,220
And he sits next to Marie Antoinette.

1237
01:01:48,620 --> 01:01:51,060
And will have quite the relationship with her.

1238
01:01:51,080 --> 01:01:51,300
Yeah.

1239
01:01:51,300 --> 01:01:52,960
He's got a slight…

1240
01:01:52,960 --> 01:01:53,540
I don't think…

1241
01:01:53,540 --> 01:01:54,600
It's not really a crush.

1242
01:01:54,600 --> 01:01:59,700
But basically, they talk a lot as the carriage rolls west.

1243
01:01:59,940 --> 01:02:02,420
And he starts to think, you know…

1244
01:02:02,420 --> 01:02:03,600
She and I have a…

1245
01:02:03,600 --> 01:02:05,660
She and I, yeah, have a really…

1246
01:02:05,660 --> 01:02:06,960
You know, we understand each other.

1247
01:02:07,000 --> 01:02:07,900
We can do business together.

1248
01:02:08,020 --> 01:02:12,300
And as we will see for Barnav and his faction who end up being called the Foyon.

1249
01:02:12,600 --> 01:02:16,260
This is a very, very bad miscalculation.

1250
01:02:17,240 --> 01:02:21,100
Because obviously, it's a reminder actually the ground is shifting so quickly.

1251
01:02:21,480 --> 01:02:24,580
That even the person who now seems to have the kind of privilege.

1252
01:02:24,600 --> 01:02:28,000
The political upper hand doesn't realize that actually…

1253
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:30,700
I mean, you know, yesterday's radical is today's centrist.

1254
01:02:30,740 --> 01:02:31,080
Exactly.

1255
01:02:31,200 --> 01:02:32,040
Well, that's exactly it.

1256
01:02:32,740 --> 01:02:34,620
Finally, they approach the Paris suburbs.

1257
01:02:34,760 --> 01:02:36,580
And the mood now is very aggressive.

1258
01:02:37,920 --> 01:02:44,140
There are attempts to storm the carriage which the National Guardsmen have to kind of fight off.

1259
01:02:44,380 --> 01:02:49,080
The scuffling between the National Guard escort and the crowds which leave people wounded.

1260
01:02:49,640 --> 01:02:51,640
And finally, they get into the city walls.

1261
01:02:51,760 --> 01:02:53,240
They're surrounded by Lafayette's cavalry.

1262
01:02:53,240 --> 01:02:55,920
And the carriage comes into the city.

1263
01:02:56,160 --> 01:02:59,080
All Paris has turned out to watch.

1264
01:02:59,720 --> 01:03:01,560
Tens of thousands of people.

1265
01:03:01,620 --> 01:03:03,100
People crammed into balconies.

1266
01:03:04,920 --> 01:03:05,960
They're on trees.

1267
01:03:06,120 --> 01:03:08,300
And they're packed into the pavements.

1268
01:03:08,680 --> 01:03:12,600
And there is just a total silence.

1269
01:03:13,100 --> 01:03:14,140
No shouting.

1270
01:03:14,460 --> 01:03:15,240
They've been told.

1271
01:03:15,400 --> 01:03:15,960
They've been ordered.

1272
01:03:16,200 --> 01:03:18,500
You know, no noise from the…

1273
01:03:18,500 --> 01:03:20,220
The orders have come down from the authorities.

1274
01:03:20,740 --> 01:03:23,220
The hats remain stubbornly.

1275
01:03:23,220 --> 01:03:23,780
On their heads.

1276
01:03:23,860 --> 01:03:26,160
They don't remove their hats when the royal carriage passes.

1277
01:03:26,560 --> 01:03:29,400
It's an atmosphere that actually is not just silent.

1278
01:03:29,500 --> 01:03:30,680
It is terrifying.

1279
01:03:31,040 --> 01:03:32,640
As the carriage comes along.

1280
01:03:33,020 --> 01:03:35,560
And at last, they reach the Tuileries Palace.

1281
01:03:36,840 --> 01:03:39,960
The guards are all crowding around them to protect them.

1282
01:03:40,760 --> 01:03:44,500
Louis and Marion Ternet and the children are able to get down and to walk inside.

1283
01:03:44,880 --> 01:03:50,960
But when the bodyguards, three noblemen, get down in their yellow coats, the crowd break through the cordon.

1284
01:03:50,960 --> 01:03:51,800
And they start…

1285
01:03:51,800 --> 01:03:55,380
They basically rip into the bodyguards and start to kind of tear them apart.

1286
01:03:56,160 --> 01:04:03,140
Lafayette's men have to kind of push back to intervene and basically drag away the battered, blooded bodyguards to safety.

1287
01:04:03,380 --> 01:04:06,460
And then the gates of the Tuileries clang shut.

1288
01:04:06,620 --> 01:04:10,040
And the doors slam shut behind Marion Ternet and Louis.

1289
01:04:10,500 --> 01:04:11,680
They're back home again.

1290
01:04:12,340 --> 01:04:13,180
And it's over.

1291
01:04:13,480 --> 01:04:14,260
They've failed.

1292
01:04:14,680 --> 01:04:17,880
And, of course, what's worse, he'd left that note.

1293
01:04:18,000 --> 01:04:19,520
And everybody has read it.

1294
01:04:19,520 --> 01:04:25,380
And it is patently clear that he will never work, you know, deep down.

1295
01:04:25,420 --> 01:04:29,720
He will never reconcile himself to the revolution and that he has been lying all along.

1296
01:04:29,920 --> 01:04:36,580
So, Dominic, just before we end this episode and the series with it, I guess one obvious question.

1297
01:04:36,740 --> 01:04:37,540
Could it have worked?

1298
01:04:37,700 --> 01:04:38,700
Could Louis have got away?

1299
01:04:39,160 --> 01:04:39,500
Yeah.

1300
01:04:39,560 --> 01:04:40,860
I think it could have worked.

1301
01:04:41,060 --> 01:04:43,120
You know, a lot of contingencies go wrong.

1302
01:04:43,240 --> 01:04:44,160
They fall behind schedule.

1303
01:04:45,220 --> 01:04:46,160
He's recognized.

1304
01:04:46,780 --> 01:04:48,740
You know, if he'd been more careful.

1305
01:04:48,740 --> 01:04:50,140
And if they'd kept to the plan.

1306
01:04:50,260 --> 01:04:52,180
Of course, you can argue that they would always have fallen behind.

1307
01:04:52,540 --> 01:04:55,820
And that, by definition, he was always going to be a bit indiscreet.

1308
01:04:56,680 --> 01:04:59,940
But the thing is, it doesn't fail by accident.

1309
01:05:00,420 --> 01:05:01,640
There is a lot of accident.

1310
01:05:01,760 --> 01:05:02,780
But it's not just accident.

1311
01:05:03,320 --> 01:05:06,000
So, there are two reasons, I think, that are crucial why it fails.

1312
01:05:06,100 --> 01:05:09,720
One is his indecision plays a massive part in the story of the French Revolution.

1313
01:05:10,520 --> 01:05:12,680
But it also plays a big part in this story.

1314
01:05:12,820 --> 01:05:18,080
Because if he had gone in 1789 when all his advisors were begging him to go.

1315
01:05:18,080 --> 01:05:18,820
Or at least some of them.

1316
01:05:19,500 --> 01:05:21,880
The chances of success would have been much higher.

1317
01:05:22,040 --> 01:05:24,600
Even in 1790, they'd have been much higher.

1318
01:05:24,740 --> 01:05:27,800
But by delaying and delaying, he makes it more likely that it fails.

1319
01:05:28,220 --> 01:05:28,740
And why?

1320
01:05:29,300 --> 01:05:30,380
This is the other thing.

1321
01:05:30,640 --> 01:05:37,280
Because La France Profonde, the countryside, has changed in ways that he doesn't ever anticipate.

1322
01:05:37,720 --> 01:05:38,620
Even in two years.

1323
01:05:38,820 --> 01:05:39,980
So, he thought it was all.

1324
01:05:40,100 --> 01:05:41,540
It's the classic thing, isn't it?

1325
01:05:41,580 --> 01:05:43,480
It's Nicholas II in St. Petersburg.

1326
01:05:43,960 --> 01:05:45,720
It's Charles I in London.

1327
01:05:46,160 --> 01:05:48,040
Oh, it's just the people in the city.

1328
01:05:48,040 --> 01:05:49,360
The other people love me.

1329
01:05:49,440 --> 01:05:50,720
Which is what Louis thought.

1330
01:05:51,220 --> 01:05:56,760
He didn't realize that even a tiny, nothing place like Varennes had changed completely.

1331
01:05:57,200 --> 01:06:04,800
And that actually when push came to shove, they would stick with the National Assembly and their own elected deputies rather than with him.

1332
01:06:04,900 --> 01:06:08,900
So, in a way, it's not just chance that stops him.

1333
01:06:09,440 --> 01:06:12,460
It's the revolution that stops him.

1334
01:06:12,500 --> 01:06:14,400
Because the revolution has changed everything.

1335
01:06:15,460 --> 01:06:18,020
And the revolution induced him.

1336
01:06:18,040 --> 01:06:23,600
And the new course now will destroy him and the royal family and the monarchy.

1337
01:06:23,720 --> 01:06:27,420
And the course is now clearly set for France to become a republic.

1338
01:06:27,800 --> 01:06:30,240
But that is a story for another time.

1339
01:06:30,320 --> 01:06:33,760
Probably in the autumn we will continue this account of the revolution.

1340
01:06:35,260 --> 01:06:38,920
But thank you for sticking with us this far.

1341
01:06:39,220 --> 01:06:40,480
We will be back.

1342
01:06:42,160 --> 01:06:43,800
Dominic, I've enjoyed doing this so much.

1343
01:06:44,140 --> 01:06:47,260
One of the great, great convulsive episodes.

1344
01:06:47,260 --> 01:06:49,820
In world history, as we've been saying.

1345
01:06:49,980 --> 01:06:52,400
And lots more convulsions still to come.

1346
01:06:52,460 --> 01:06:55,480
Yeah, we'll be back in the autumn with the fall of the monarchy.

1347
01:06:55,620 --> 01:06:56,960
The outbreak of war.

1348
01:06:57,960 --> 01:06:58,840
The guillotine.

1349
01:06:59,500 --> 01:07:00,900
The beginning of the reign of terror.

1350
01:07:01,140 --> 01:07:01,960
Murder of Marat.

1351
01:07:02,420 --> 01:07:03,660
So much to come.

1352
01:07:03,660 --> 01:07:04,540
Lots of exciting things.

1353
01:07:05,920 --> 01:07:08,360
And thank you so much for sticking with us on this.

1354
01:07:08,580 --> 01:07:13,260
And our next episode will be something completely different.

1355
01:07:14,360 --> 01:07:17,240
Namely, the faking of a prehistoric skeleton.

1356
01:07:17,240 --> 01:07:19,800
In early 20th century Sussex.

1357
01:07:20,040 --> 01:07:21,580
We'll be looking at the Piltdown Man.

1358
01:07:22,640 --> 01:07:25,420
And then after that we'll be looking at the history of Italian food.

1359
01:07:25,680 --> 01:07:28,260
So a slight palate cleanser there.

1360
01:07:28,280 --> 01:07:29,160
Definitely a palate cleanser.

1361
01:07:29,220 --> 01:07:30,600
But for now, à bientôt.

1362
01:07:31,120 --> 01:07:31,720
Au revoir.

